      "dom_id": "question-11124",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+2 \\\\ y & =-x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0+3 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,3).$",
