      "dom_id": "question-47406",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $(2,8)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2)=4(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-8 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-64 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8y & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=8+66=74.$",
