      "dom_id": "question-342187",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-\\frac{2}{x}$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=4x-\\frac{2}{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4+2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=4+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=4+2=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{6}[x-(-1)] \\\\ -6(y+2) & =x+1 \\\\ -6y-12 & =x+1 \\\\ x+6\\cdot y & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
